DESCRIPTION:Department of Art and Art History\n\n \n\nLump Presents: UNC-Ch
 apel Hill MFA Thesis Exhibitions \n\nMarch 6th - March 29th:\n\nINFRA ULTR
 A by CJ Murphy / Under the Thumb by Michelle Lisa Polissaint\n\nOpening re
 ception: March 6\, 6-9 pm\n\n \n\nApril 3rd - April 26th:\n\nNepantla by W
 endy Yamilett Moran / Growing Elsewhere: Ghorbatī (غربتی) by Rojano 
 Mohammadzadeh / Blood\, Sap\, Dirt by Iris Moyan Wang\n\nOpening reception
 : April 3\, 6-9 pm\n\nLump Gallery is located at 505 S. Blount St.\, Ralei
 gh\, NC 27601\n\nOpening Hours: Saturdays and Sundays\, 1-5 pm\, and by ap
 pointment.\n\n \n\nLump Gallery is pleased to present thesis exhibitions b
 y UNC-Chapel Hill’s 2026 MFA graduating class. \n\n \n\nAt the conclusio
 n of a rigorous\, research-based two-year program\, these artists consider
  the concept of place and their personal relationships to it. Each artist 
 employs a range of media and responds to geographic\, constructed\, techno
 logical\, and bodily autonomy. These relationships between place and perso
 nal narrative raise broader questions around positionality and society\; c
 ommunication and identity\; and human impact. Revelation\, purpose\, and p
 ossibility guide each artist’s research-centered practices.\n\n \n\nCJ M
 urphy creates works that render the intersection of technology\, magic\, a
 nd globalization material by reinvigorating found and discarded electrical
  waste. Michelle Lisa Polissaint draws on her experience in a strict relig
 ious community and deep research into the BITE method to discuss contempor
 ary issues of indoctrination\, high-control groups\, and authoritarianism.
  Rojano Mohammadzadeh draws on Islamic prayer\, talismanic imagery\, text\
 , and ritualized gestures to approach the immigrant experience through wha
 t she terms transplantation: survival through negotiating acceptance withi
 n a host body. Wendy Yamilett Morán’s practice explores the intersectio
 ns of culture\, realism\, and spirituality through painting and ceramics\,
  using their work to examine how identity\, memory\, and belonging are sha
 ped within in-between spaces. Iris Moyan Wang is an interdisciplinary arti
 st working primarily with ink painting and ceramics to create fluid and er
 otic hybridizations of bodies and botanicals that reimagine Chinese cultur
 al heritage.\n\n \n\nFor press inquiries or other questions\, please conta
 ct uncmfathesis@yahoo.com
GEO:35.773769;-78.636571
LOCATION:Lump Gallery/Projects
SUMMARY:MFA 2026 Thesis Exhibitions at Lump Gallery
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/mfa-2026-thesis-exhibitions-at
 -lump-gallery
CATEGORIES:Exhibits
END:VEVENT
