DTSTAMP:20260519T042241Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52897136307343
DTSTART:20260521T180000Z
DTEND:20260521T190000Z
DESCRIPTION:Recruitment materials are often treated as neutral tools\, but 
 they actively shape who chooses to participate—and why. In qualitative a
 nd decentralized research\, where trust and authenticity are critical\, po
 orly aligned design choices can unintentionally attract insincere particip
 ants while deterring the very individuals a study aims to reach.\n\n \n\nT
 his session explores how recruitment materials function as signals\, influ
 encing perceptions of legitimacy\, eligibility\, and risk. Through real-wo
 rld case examples and interactive discussion\, participants will examine h
 ow design elements—such as language\, compensation framing\, and screeni
 ng structure—can create vulnerability to misrepresentation. Attendees wi
 ll leave with practical strategies to align recruitment approaches with st
 udy risk tolerance\, strengthening both participant authenticity and data 
 integrity.\n\n \n\nPresenter:\nSarah Sutter\, MPH\nResearch Project Manage
 r\, Recruitment & Retention Program\nNC TraCS Institute\n\n \n\nRegister
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:Insincere Participation: What Your Recruitment Design Is Really Say
 ing (and Who It's Inviting In)
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/insincere-participation-what-y
 our-recruitment-design-is-really-saying-and-who-its-inviting-in
CATEGORIES:Workshops and Classes
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260519T042241Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52754256709167
DTSTART:20260521T223000Z
DTEND:20260522T000000Z
DESCRIPTION:Since the earliest days of human history\, the sky has inspired
  wonder\, curiosity\, and meaning.\n\nWhat can our relationship with the s
 ky still teach us today?\n\n  \n\nAcross cultures and centuries\, humanity
 ’s fascination with the heavens has shaped religion\, poetry\, literatur
 e\, music\, art\, navigation\, timekeeping\, and the ways we understand ou
 rselves and our place in the world. That connection is still very much ali
 ve.\n\n \n\nJoin Professor Sheila Kannappan for a visually rich and though
 t-provoking journey through humanity’s enduring relationship with the sk
 y. Tracing our celestial awareness from the earliest emergence of Homo sap
 iens to the present day\, this presentation invites us to reflect on how d
 eeply astronomical awe is woven into human culture\, not just as a relic o
 f the past\, but as a vital perspective for modern life.\n\n \n\nThrough t
 his lens\, we will explore the values the cosmos can inspire\, including o
 penness\, connectedness\, growth\, and humility\, and why they may matter 
 now more than ever.\n\n \n\nCome rediscover the sky\, and perhaps\, a rene
 wed sense of wonder!\n\n \n\nRegistration is required to attend this free 
 event! \n\nCampus Life Experience (CLE) credits are available to UNC stude
 nts via https://heellife.unc.edu/
GEO:35.899895;-79.012764
LOCATION:Friday Center
SUMMARY:We Need the Sky: Awe and Our Connection to the Heavens
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/we-need-the-sky-awe-and-our-co
 nnection-to-the-heavens
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
