DESCRIPTION:The Wellness Exchange is an immersive\, interactive wellness sy
 mposium for people with cancer\, their caregivers\, and their adult family
  members. During the Wellness Exchange\, participants will have the opport
 unity to learn from guest speakers\, laugh with a comedian\, practice well
 ness through art and writing therapy\, move their bodies with yoga and exe
 rcise\, and rest through meditation\, mindfulness\, and reiki. During lunc
 h\, we will hear from NFL Legend Chris Draft and winningest ACC women's ba
 sketball coach Sylvia Hatchell. Participants will choose the sessions they
  wish to attend during the day. Breakfast and lunch will be served.\n\n \n
 \nFor registration and more informaton visit: https://www.activeaftercance
 r.org/wellnessexchange or contact Carly Bailey- Carly_Bailey@med.unc.edu
GEO:35.899895;-79.012764
LOCATION:The Friday Center
SUMMARY:The Wellness Exchange- Interactive Cancer Wellness Symposium 
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/the-wellness-exchange-interact
 ive-cancer-wellness-symposium
CATEGORIES:Workshops and Classes
CATEGORIES:Meetings and Conferences
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260519T042241Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52638987481885
DTSTART:20260909T130000Z
DTEND:20260909T190000Z
DESCRIPTION:This course will take place over two days (9/9/2026 and 9/11/20
 26) and will be offered via Zoom. Attendance is required as the course wil
 l not be recorded.\n\nNesting can arise from hierarchical data structures 
 (e.g.\, siblings nested within family\; patients nested within therapist)\
 , longitudinal data structures (repeated measures nested within individual
 )\, or both (repeated measures nested within patient and patient nested wi
 thin therapist).\n\nIt is well known that the analysis of nested data stru
 ctures using traditional general linear models (e.g.\, ANOVA or regression
 ) is flawed\, oftentimes substantially so: Tests of significance are likel
 y biased and within- and between-group effects are confounded with one ano
 ther. All of these limitations can be addressed within the multilevel mode
 l.\n\nIn this workshop\, we provide an introduction to the application of 
 multilevel models with nested data\, including software implementation in 
 SAS\, SPSS and Stata.\n\nRegistration and fee required for Zoom link acces
 s.
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:Introduction to Multilevel Modeling 
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/introduction-to-multilevel-mod
 eling-6356
CATEGORIES:Workshops and Classes
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260519T042241Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52638987482910
DTSTART:20260911T130000Z
DTEND:20260911T190000Z
DESCRIPTION:This course will take place over two days (9/9/2026 and 9/11/20
 26) and will be offered via Zoom. Attendance is required as the course wil
 l not be recorded.\n\nNesting can arise from hierarchical data structures 
 (e.g.\, siblings nested within family\; patients nested within therapist)\
 , longitudinal data structures (repeated measures nested within individual
 )\, or both (repeated measures nested within patient and patient nested wi
 thin therapist).\n\nIt is well known that the analysis of nested data stru
 ctures using traditional general linear models (e.g.\, ANOVA or regression
 ) is flawed\, oftentimes substantially so: Tests of significance are likel
 y biased and within- and between-group effects are confounded with one ano
 ther. All of these limitations can be addressed within the multilevel mode
 l.\n\nIn this workshop\, we provide an introduction to the application of 
 multilevel models with nested data\, including software implementation in 
 SAS\, SPSS and Stata.\n\nRegistration and fee required for Zoom link acces
 s.
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:Introduction to Multilevel Modeling 
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/introduction-to-multilevel-mod
 eling-6356
CATEGORIES:Workshops and Classes
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260519T042241Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52389476244944
DTSTART:20260914T140000Z
DTEND:20260914T160000Z
DESCRIPTION:This course will be held online only over 3 days (September 14\
 , 16\, and 18). Attendance is required as it will not be recorded.\n\nThis
  6-hour course provides training on how AI can assist a survey project fro
 m beginning to the end.\n\nWe will focus on each step of the research proc
 ess (Callegaro and Yang\, 2025)\n\nPlanningExecutionActivationIn the plann
 ing phase there are two sub phases\n1) Ideate and define\n\nAI can be an i
 dea helper and assist on generating research ideas starting from previousl
 y knowledgeA literature review can definitely be assisted by AI and there 
 are specific tools available for that2) Design and plan\n\nThe design stag
 e is where AI can assist in producing design brief\, analysis plan and map
  the concepts that are going to be measured using the questionnaireThe exe
 cution phase consists of\n3) Data wizard\n\nHere AI has been used to help 
 drafting and pretesting the questionnaire\, help writing survey material s
 uch as survey invitation and confidentiality assurances.4) Analysis copilo
 t\n\nOnce the data collection is under way\, AI can assist in automating\,
  and writing statistical code to analyze the dataset (stat code assistant)
 . It can also be used as  theme discovery\, for coding of open ended answe
 rs and data visualization.The activation phase consists of:\n5) Insight pa
 rtner\n\nHelp summarize findings\, generate reports\, help review the fina
 l deliverables such as a research report or a complete academic paper6) St
 orytelling aid\n\nDevelop presentation materials\, create multi-media arti
 facts\, generated blog post for different platforms\, generate images base
 d on content from the researchOrganizational Structure of the Course \nThe
  course will be taught in a workshop style where the lectures will be usin
 g presentation slides as a roadmap. After each concept has been introduced
  and discussed\, the participants will be invited to work on brief hands o
 n exercises with different LLMs such as Gemini\, Claude\, Copilot\, and Ch
 atGPT\n\nParticipants will be strongly encouraged to ask questions during 
 lectures\, especially on how the content relates to their own research.\n\
 nLearning objectives\nBy the end of the course\, you will:\n\nLearn how to
  prompt LLMs for survey research help and assistanceLearn how to evaluate 
 the quality of LLMs outputsStrengths and challenges of different LLMs tool
 sWhere LLMs are not necessaryEthical usage of LLMs/AI ToolsRecent debate o
 f using LLMs to produce research and write academic papersRegistration and
  fee (refundable for UNCCH students) are required for Zoom link access.
